    rdfs:label "description] tikzcd% The Music publication factorization law as a commutative diagram: the direct dashed arrow \\(SourcePacket\\) is forbidden; the only admissible path is \\(render\\).fig:music-publication-factorization theorem[Publication factorization] thm:music-publication-factorization In the Music engine, every publication arrow \\(p:SourcePacket\\) must factor as \\[ p = render. \\] The direct arrow \\(SourcePacket\\) violates the Node-or-Arrow law unless this factorization is explicit. theorem proof Step 1 (symbols). \\(normalize\\) is the typing arrow, \\(validate\\) is the shape-gate arrow, \\(render\\) is the publication arrow. Step 2 (operations). Each step is an olog arrow in the Music category; composition is pipeline execution. Step 3 (base topic). Category composition and the Node-or-Arrow law license the factorization: every arrow in the page olog must have a typed source and target. Step 4 (concrete anchor). For the Mittens body-mass observation: the raw weight record is normalized to a \\(TypedQualityMeasurement\\), validated by the SHACL measurement shape, and rendered to a published measurement section. No direct jump from weight record to section is admitted. Step 5 (Yoneda). By Yoneda, the profile of \\(PublishedEntry\\) at \\((-,PublishedEntry)\\) recovers the full normalized, validated, rendered chain for every incoming arrow. Step 6 (recap). The factorization \\(p=render\\) is the Music admission law. proof Diagram law (Figure~fig:music-publication-factorization): the left shortcut path and the right full path must agree at \\(PublishedEntry\\); equality is the statement that the shortcut is only admissible as the displayed composite. [0.92]% tikzcd[column sep=large,row sep=large,ampersand replacement=\\&] |[schemanode]| TaskExtract [r," .
